the one-year anniversary of the january  6th insurrection has passed but a new  collection at the smithsonian is  documenting how the day will be  historically preserved for future  generations the exhibit is still in the  early stages congressional correspondent  nicole killian got a glimpse and sat  down with lawmakers who whose memorable  actions have inspired part of the  curation  there's not a day that goes by when i'm  at the capitol where i don't think about  it it's still hard for new jersey  congressman andy kim to shake the images  of january 6. i remember walking into  the house oh into the rotunda and  it just my heart sank so his instinct  kicked in i just started to to clean up  there was a lot of just trash i mean  clothing  flags  what really compelled me to do it was  when my mother brought me to show me the  capitol building so it was personal very  i i  i hurt so much and i just remember  thinking like is there  anything i can do to try to have our  country move forward on this and i  didn't want to see a sunrise on a broken  capital  the image of kim in his blue j crew suit  instantly went viral and is now part of  a collection that will eventually go on  display at the smithsonian you don't  always associate political history with  clothing claire jerry is curator at the  museum of american history which  unveiled some of its items from  political signs retrieved right after  the riot to law enforcement badges and a  slashed bulletproof vest  how do you make this more than just a  terrible moment in history  well one of the ways we've done that i  think is by looking at other types of  events it is i think incumbent upon us  for future historians researchers and  for the american people to say there  were a lot of things happening this day  there's a lot of things that happen in  history and not all of them are easy to  talk about do you think congress has  healed no not at all in fact has become  in some ways a  moment that has divided us even further  maybe that exhibit can be a first step  i think certainly  understanding the truth of that day  needs to be a first step you cannot have  reconciliation unless you have the truth  to set the country free for cbs saturday  morning nicole killian washington
 

